{"id":"renaissance-revival","title":"Renaissance Revival","url":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Renaissance_Revival","rev":238468,"fetched":"2026-10-16","lead":[{"t":"p","c":[{"t":"b","c":["Renaissance Revival"]}," refers to a trend originating from the 1990s that reinterprets elements from fashion and art of the Renaissance through a contemporary lens. It is defined by imagery reminiscent of the Renaissance era, drawing inspiration from fairytales, Greco-Roman paintings and art styles from the Middle Ages and Elizabethan period. The aesthetic often uses warm muted colors, sepia filters/textures and renaissance script fonts to evoke a sense of antique quality."]}],"sections":[{"title":"Visuals","id":"visuals","blocks":[{"t":"p","c":["The aesthetic uses muted colors and lighting to make the visuals appear decayed and vintage. The palette consists of sepia tones of gold, brown, and beige."]}]},{"title":"Fashion","id":"fashion","blocks":[{"t":"p","c":["Renaissance Revival is based on the silhouettes of the era. Common attire includes corsets, Elizabethan ruffle collars, bodices and long dresses."]}]},{"title":"Media","id":"media","blocks":[{"t":"h","l":3,"c":["Movies"]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":["Queen Margot (1994)"]},{"c":["Romeo + Juliet (1996)"]},{"c":["Dangerous Beauty (1998)"]},{"c":["Elizabeth (1998)"]},{"c":["Ever After (1998)"]},{"c":["Shakespeare in Love (1998)"]},{"c":["A Midsummer Night's Dream (1999)"]},{"c":["The Messenger: The Story of Joan of Arc (1999)"]},{"c":["Get Over It (2001)"]}]}]},{"title":"Gallery","id":"gallery","blocks":[{"t":"gallery","items":[{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/2/2b/Loreena_mckennitt.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260118045424","w":185,"h":185},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/0/07/Mask_and_mirror.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20260118045443","w":185,"h":185},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/e/eb/A_celebration_of_women_in_music.jpg/revision/latest?cb=202601